<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tikal</h1><p class="st-meta">90 CE – 900 CE (approx.) · capital Tikal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the most powerful Classic Maya kingdoms, Mutal, whose temples rise above the Petén forest and whose long rivalry with Calakmul shaped Maya politics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tikal was occupied from about 800 BCE and grew into a major centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-preclassic">Late Preclassic</a>. Its royal dynasty, later counted back to the founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yax-ehb-xook">Yax Ehb Xook</a>, is traditionally dated to about 90 CE, and the North Acropolis became its royal burial ground.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 378 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaj-kak">Sihyaj K'ahk'</a>, linked with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan</a>, arrived and installed a new king, after which Tikal dominated much of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>. Its rivalry with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a> led to defeat by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caracol">Caracol</a> in 562 and a long hiatus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasaw-chan-kawiil-i">Jasaw Chan K'awiil I</a> defeated Calakmul in 695, opening a revival marked by the great twin-pyramid complexes and Temples I to VI.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tikal's last dated monument was raised in 869, and the city was largely abandoned by about 900 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-maya-collapse">Classic Maya collapse</a>. It became a national park in 1955 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tikal.jpg" alt="Tikal" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Mundo Maya · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tikal_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>90 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tikal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Siyaj Chan K'awiil II, Jasaw Chan K'awiil I, Yik'in Chan K'awiil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mutal, Yax Mutal, Kingdom of Mutal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=495&amp;focus=tikal" aria-label="Tikal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2244 1538 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tikal lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2564 1794L2565 1788M2564 1794L2541 1823"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2565 1788h0M2541 1823h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2564 1794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2564 1794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=495&amp;focus=tikal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

